Thanks, the v2->v3 changelog shows the earlier Yao Zi review feedback (header
reorder, simplifying channel-attrs structs to u32 arrays, dropping null
checks before free()) was addressed.

A couple of things in arch/riscv/include/asm/sbi.h:

- sbi_to_linux_error() has a `default:` case alongside explicit
  `case SBI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED:` / `case SBI_ERR_FAILURE:` - since both of
  those map to -EOPNOTSUPP same as default, the explicit cases are
  redundant with default but harmless documentation of intent; not a
  blocker, just noting it in case you want to drop them for brevity or keep
  them for clarity (your call).

- rpmi_sbi_mpxy_read_attrs() loops `for (i = 0; i < attr_count; i++)` where
  `i` is declared `int` and `attr_count` is `u32` - this is a signed/unsigned
  comparison that most compilers won't warn on by default, but worth using
  `u32 i` to be consistent with the rest of the file's u32 usage.

Is this series intended to land together with a corresponding client driver
change (rpmi-uclass.c) that actually selects/uses RPMI_SBI_MPXY, or is this
transport meant to be usable standalone once an RPMI_FIRMWARE consumer
exists in-tree? Worth clarifying in the cover letter for context on test
coverage.
